INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S ROOM - NIGHT

A round stone room. An iron stove glows. A kettle ticks. Outside, a gale tears at the shutters.

INSPECTOR MALCOLM REYNOLDS, fifties, in an oilskin coat, sits at a rough table. He winds the spool of a portable dictaphone and presses PLAY.

DICTAPHONE (V.O.)
(his own voice, calm)
Entry one. The keeper is dead.

He pours tea. He is not troubled.

DICTAPHONE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Entry two. The boat was sabotaged, the radio smashed, and the logbook burned.

He sips. He turns a page of the logbook, very much unburned.

DICTAPHONE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Entry three. No one else is on the island.

The shutters bang. He stops the machine. He looks up at the winding iron stairs, listening. A single drop falls from the ceiling and hits the table.

Slowly, he rewinds the spool.

REYNOLDS
(quietly)
Entry four.
